---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import UiLabel from "../components/UiLabel.astro";
import WebAppLink from "../components/WebAppLink.astro";

// Issue #1102: the account-deletion page Google Play's Data safety form
// links to from its "Delete account URL" field. Every factual claim about
// what deletion does carries a `cite:` comment naming the source of record
// (PRIVACY.md §7, or the code that ships it), per the issue's acceptance
// criterion; test/site/store_pages_test.dart pins those citations.
//
// Two inputs are deliberately pending (issue #1100 — the owner picks the
// public support address): the email address itself and the committed
// response time. They are marked as pending below rather than invented;
// until they land, this page points at the contact address already
// published in PRIVACY.md. Do not point the Play Console at this URL until
// that follow-up has merged.

const privacyPolicyUrl =
  "https://github.com/wjdavis5/lunarlog/blob/main/PRIVACY.md#7-data-export--retention--deletion-rights";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Delete your account"
  description="How to delete your lunarlog account — from inside the app on iPhone, Android, or the browser, or by email without the app — and exactly what is and isn't deleted."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1 class="doc__title">Delete your account</h1>
    <p class="doc__lede">
      You can delete your lunarlog account yourself, from inside the app, and
      it happens immediately — this is not a request we review later. This page
      explains how, and exactly what is and isn't deleted.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>Delete from inside the app</h2>
      <!-- cite: lib/ui/settings/settings_screen.dart (Settings screen, Account section), lib/ui/account/account_section.dart:562 (the "Delete account" tile), lib/ui/components/app_shell.dart (the More tab hosts Settings). The steps are identical on iPhone and Android; the browser version has its own, below. -->
      <p>
        The steps are the same on iPhone and Android. The
        <a href="#browser">browser version</a> has its own, a little shorter.
      </p>
      <!-- cite: lib/domain/account/account_deletion_service.dart (AccountDeletionFailure.appleNativeCeremonyUnavailable: no Sign in with Apple step on this platform); lib/ui/account/account_section.dart (the failure's copy is shown and nothing is deleted); lib/l10n/app_en.arb (accountDeletionAppleCeremonyUnavailable). The gap is tracked as issue 665. -->
      <p>
        One exception: an account that has Sign in with Apple linked cannot
        finish deleting on Android, because Android has no Sign in with
        Apple step for it to confirm with. The app says so and deletes
        nothing. Delete that account from an iPhone or iPad, from the
        <a href="#browser">browser version</a>, or
        <a href="#by-email">by email</a>.
      </p>
      <ol class="doc__steps">
        <li>Open lunarlog and sign in to the account you want to delete.</li>
        <li>
          Open <strong>More</strong> in the bottom bar to reach
          <strong>Settings</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Scroll to the <strong>Account</strong> section and tap
          <strong>Delete account</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>
          The confirmation dialog tells you exactly what will be deleted and
          names the profiles it will take with it. Before confirming you can
          also:
          <ul>
            <li>
              choose <strong>Export first</strong> to save a JSON copy of your
              data, or export any time earlier from
              <strong>Settings → Your data</strong>; and
            </li>
            <li>
              choose <strong>Transfer ownership first</strong> if you own a
              profile that other people still use — see
              <a href="#minor-profiles">a minor's profile</a> below.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
          If your account owns profiles shared with other guardians, tick the
          acknowledgement — it confirms you understand those guardians lose
          access.
        </li>
        <li>
          Tap <strong>Delete account</strong> to finish. Your server data and
          your account are normally gone by the time the confirmation
          completes, and the copy on this device is wiped with them.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion" ("This is not a request queued for later…"). -->
      <p>
        Deletion is permanent and cannot be undone. If you think you may want
        your history later, export first.
      </p>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion" (the fail-closed rule for Apple revocation and attachment cleanup). -->
      <p>
        One safety detail: if something fails part way — an Apple
        confirmation that doesn't land, a file that can't be removed — the
        deletion stops instead of half-completing, and you can retry it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="browser">
      <h2>Delete from the browser version</h2>
      <!-- cite: webapp/src/App.tsx (the header's Account link, shown once signed in); webapp/src/pages/AccountPage.tsx (the Account page: the export card, and DeleteAccountCard with its confirmation step and the Apple turn); supabase/functions/delete-account/index.ts (the same function the phone apps call). Labels via UiLabel from lib/l10n/app_en.arb. -->
      <p>
        The browser version at
        <WebAppLink /> has no More
        tab. Deletion is on its account page:
      </p>
      <ol class="doc__steps">
        <li>Sign in to the account you want to delete.</li>
        <li>
          Open <UiLabel key="accountSectionTitle" /> in the header.
        </li>
        <li>
          To keep a copy, use <UiLabel key="yourDataExportTitle" /> on that
          page first.
        </li>
        <li>
          Under <UiLabel key="accountSectionDelete" />, choose
          <UiLabel key="accountSectionDelete" />. The page says what will be
          deleted and asks you to confirm; choose
          <UiLabel key="accountDeleteDialogConfirm" /> again to finish.
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        If the account signs in with Apple, you are sent to Apple to confirm
        before anything is deleted. Deleting from the browser deletes the
        same account and the same data as deleting from a phone.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is deleted</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion"; supabase/functions/delete-account/index.ts; supabase/migrations/20260905110000_account_deletion.sql (delete_account_data()). -->
      <ul>
        <li>
          Every profile you own, and every entry logged on them — including
          entries other guardians logged on those profiles.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your settings, your guardian memberships on other people's profiles,
          and every invitation you created.
        </li>
        <li>
          Your minimum-age acknowledgement record, and the progress record for
          any bulk import you ran (a source label and row counts — no health
          content).
        </li>
        <li>
          Your feedback tickets, their reply threads, and any attached
          screenshots.
        </li>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Prediction-Only Sharing Excludes Minors" ("deleted from our servers the moment the connection is revoked or the account or profile is deleted"). -->
        <li>
          Any prediction-sharing snapshot you were sharing; it is removed from
          the servers with the account.
        </li>
        <li>
          The account itself — including, if you signed in with Apple, that
          app's access being revoked through Apple.
        </li>
        <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §6 "Browser Client (Web)" (nothing at rest in the browser); webapp/src/lib/authQueries.ts (useDeleteAccount drops the page's data and session). -->
        <li>
          The copy of your data on this device. The browser version keeps no
          copy to begin with, so deleting your account there clears what the
          open tab was showing and signs it out.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What is kept</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion"; the delete dialog's guardian note (lib/l10n/app_en.arb accountDeleteDialogGuardianNote); delete_account_data() step 0 in supabase/migrations/20260905110000_account_deletion.sql. -->
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Profiles owned by other people survive untouched.</strong> If
          you were a guardian on someone else's profile, only your membership
          ends — their profile and its history are not affected.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Entries you logged on those profiles stay</strong>, with
          their day-to-day ownership reassigned to the profile's owner. Your
          name stops being shown as the person who logged them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Writes already mirrored to Apple Health or Health
          Connect</strong> stay in your device's own health store. Deleting
          your lunarlog account does not reach into it.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Shared profiles</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion"; the delete dialog's blast-radius copy and acknowledgement (lib/ui/account/delete_account_dialog.dart, lib/l10n/app_en.arb accountDeleteDialogBlastRadiusGuardians / accountDeleteDialogAck). -->
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>A profile you own that others can see:</strong> deleted
          completely, with its whole history. The other guardians — a partner,
          a co-parent, a caregiver — lose access to it. Their own accounts are
          not affected, but the shared profile is gone and this cannot be
          undone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A profile shared with you that someone else owns:</strong>
          keeps its history. Your access ends; the entries you logged remain
          with the profile, re-attributed to its owner as described above.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="minor-profiles">
      <h2>A minor's profile</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §5 "Two-stage custodian model" ("A parent who deletes their own account no longer removes a profile they have transferred away; conversely, if the child later deletes their own account, the profile and its history go with it"); the dialog acknowledgement (accountDeleteDialogAck). -->
      <p>
        A profile a parent created for a child starts under the parent's
        account, and can be transferred to the child's own account when she is
        ready to hold it:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Already transferred:</strong> the profile belongs to the
          child's account now. Deleting the parent's account does not touch
          it — the profile and its whole history stay with the child.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Not yet transferred:</strong> the profile is deleted with the
          parent's account, history included. The app asks you to acknowledge
          this specifically before it will delete an account that owns minor
          profiles.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you own a minor's profile you do not want to lose, use
        <strong>Transfer ownership first</strong> in the delete dialog — or
        transfer ownership from the profile's settings at any time before
        deleting.
      </p>
      <p>
        The same rule applies to any account holder: whoever owns a profile
        takes it with them when they delete their account.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Deleting less than everything</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Local Deletion" and "Support Ticket Retention"; docs/ops/store-declarations.md §5 "Single-ticket narrower path". -->
      <p>You don't have to close your account to remove things:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Single entries or whole profiles</strong> can be deleted from
          inside the app at any time, without closing your account.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A single feedback ticket</strong> can be deleted on request
          (see the email path below) while keeping your account: the ticket and
          its reply thread are removed. One honest caveat — a screenshot
          attached to a ticket deleted this way currently needs your account to
          still exist when we process the request, so the file can be removed
          in the same pass. Deleting your whole account always removes tickets
          and screenshots itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your data can be exported</strong> at any time from
          <strong>Settings → Your data</strong> — with or without an account,
          online or offline.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="by-email">
      <h2>Without the app</h2>
      <!-- cite: PRIVACY.md §7 "Account & Cloud Deletion" ("You can still reach us at … for any deletion the in-app flow does not cover"); pending inputs are issue #1100's. -->
      <p>
        If you no longer have access to the app or the device — or you need a
        deletion the in-app flow doesn't cover — you can request deletion by
        email instead.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>What to include:</strong> the email address your lunarlog
          account uses. Please don't include health details — we don't need
          them, and email isn't the place for them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>What happens next:</strong> because this path permanently
          deletes an account, we take a verification step to make sure the
          request really is yours before acting.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <aside class="note note--pending">
        <span class="note__label">Pending publication</span>
        <p>
          The dedicated address for these requests, the exact verification
          step, and the response time we commit to are being finalized and
          will be published here. Until then, the contact address already
          listed in
          <a href={privacyPolicyUrl}>our privacy policy (Section 7)</a> works
          for deletion requests.
        </p>
      </aside>
    </section>

    <p class="doc__footer-note">
      This page summarizes Section 7 of
      <a href={privacyPolicyUrl}>our privacy policy</a> and the deletion code
      that ships in the app. If anything here ever disagrees with the policy,
      the policy wins — and please tell us.
    </p>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
